To make sure that your WordPress blog post images appear when shared on Facebook, you can follow these steps:

1. Add an Open Graph meta tag to your WordPress theme or plugin. This will provide Facebook with the necessary information about the image to display. You can use the Yoast SEO plugin for this purpose.
2. Check if the image is publicly accessible. Make sure that the image file is located in a public directory and has appropriate permissions (usually 644).
3. Use a consistent image format. Facebook supports JPEG, PNG, and GIF formats. Make sure that you use the same format throughout your site.
4. Resize the image appropriately. Facebook recommends using images that are at least 1200 x 630 pixels. However, you can also use smaller images as long as they are proportionate to the original size.
5. Test your post on Facebook's Sharing Debugger. This tool allows you to preview how your post will appear on Facebook and identify any issues with the image.

If you continue to experience issues, you can try adding the following code to your theme's functions.php file or a site-specific plugin:

```php
add_filter('the_content', 'fb_open_graph_meta');
function fb_open_graph_meta($content) {
    $og_image = '';
    if (has_post_thumbnail()) {
        $og_image = get_the_post_thumbnail_url(get_the_ID(), 'full');
    }
    if ($og_image) {
        $og_image = '<meta property="og:image" content="' . $og_image . '" />';
    }
    return $og_image . $content;
}
```

This code will add an Open Graph meta tag to your blog posts, which should help Facebook display the correct image when sharing your content.

Here's a JavaScript function that accepts text and posts messages via the Slack API to a specified channel:

```javascript
function postToSlack(text, channel) {
  const url = `https://slack.com/api/chat.postMessage?token=YOUR_SLACK_API_TOKEN&channel=${channel}&text=${encodeURIComponent(text)}`;

  fetch(url, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'
    }
  })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => console.log('Message posted successfully:', data))
    .catch(error => console.error('Error posting message:', error));
}

// Example usage
postToSlack('Hello, world!', '#general');
```

Replace `YOUR_SLACK_API_TOKEN` with your actual Slack API token. You can obtain an API token by following these instructions: https://slack.com/help/articles/1150052681343-Creating-and-using-API-tokens

Also, replace `#general` with the desired channel name or ID. You can find the channel ID by hovering over the channel name in the Slack web interface and looking at the URL in the address bar. The channel ID is the number after `/#`.

This function uses the fetch API to send a POST request to the Slack API with the provided text and channel. It then logs the response data to the console. If there's an error, it logs the error message to the console as well.

