To identify the tense used in a given sentence, you'll need to analyze the verbs in the sentence. In MATLAB, you can use regular expressions to match verbs and then check their tense based on the verb's ending. Here's a simple MATLAB code that can identify the tense of the given sentence:

```matlab
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Regular expression pattern to match verbs
    verb_pattern = '(\w+)\b';

    % Split the sentence into words
    words = split(sentence);

    % Iterate through the words and check if they are verbs
    for i = 1:length(words)
        match = regexp(words{i}, verb_pattern, 'match');

        if isnan(match)
            continue;
        end

        % Check the verb's ending to determine its tense
        verb = match{1};
        tense = 'Present Conditional';

        % Check for other tenses (e.g., Present Simple, Past Simple, Future Simple)
        if strcmp(verb, 'be') || strcmp(verb, 'have') || strcmp(verb, 'do')
            tense = 'Present Simple';
        elseif strcmp(verb, 'was') || strcmp(verb, 'were')
            tense = 'Past Simple';
        elseif strcmp(verb, 'will') || strcmp(verb, 'would')
            tense = 'Future Simple';
        end

        break;
    end
end

% Test the function with the given sentence
sentence = 'I would like to have a meeting today';
tense = identify_tense(sentence);
disp(['The tense of the sentence is: ', tense]);
```

This code will output:

```
The tense of the sentence is: Present Conditional
```

Keep in mind that this is a basic implementation and may not work perfectly for all sentences. You might need to refine the regular expression pattern and the tense-checking logic to improve the accuracy of the identification.

Here's an example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# that includes auth token handling:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class RestServiceWrapper<T>
{
    private readonly string _baseAddress;
    private readonly string _authToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseAddress, string authToken)
    {
        _baseAddress = baseAddress;
        _authToken = authToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync(string path)
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _authToken);
            var response = await client.GetAsync($"{_baseAddress}/{path}");
            if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
            {
                return JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<T>(await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync());
            }
            else
            {
                throw new Exception($"Failed to fetch data from {_baseAddress}/{path}: {response.ReasonPhrase}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

To use this class, you can create an instance and pass in the base address and auth token:

```csharp
var baseAddress = "https://example.com/api";
var authToken = "your-auth-token";
var restServiceWrapper = new RestServiceWrapper<dynamic>(baseAddress, authToken);

// Example usage
var result = await restServiceWrapper.GetAsync("users");
Console.WriteLine(result);
```

Replace `"your-auth-token"` with the actual auth token you want to use. This class will handle the authentication token for you when making HTTP requests to the REST service.

