Now, let's write the C# code to train and implement the Naive Bayes algorithm for text classification:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Classification;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Features;
using Accord.Math.Random;

namespace TextClassificationNaiveBayes
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Load the dataset
            var data = File.ReadAllLines("dataset.txt").Select(line => line.Split('\t')).ToList();

            // Create a Naive Bayes classifier
            var naiveBayes = new NaiveBayes();

            // Preprocess the data
            var vectorizer = new CountVectorizer();
            var features = vectorizer.Transform(data);

            // Fit the model on the labeled dataset
            naiveBayes.Fit(features, data.Select(d => d[1]));

            // Classify a new sentence
            string sentence = "The sports game was exciting.";
            var featuresNew = vectorizer.Transform(new[] { sentence });
            var prediction = naiveBayes.Predict(featuresNew);

            Console.WriteLine($"Predicted label: {prediction}");
        }
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace `"dataset.txt"` with the path to your dataset file, which should have the following format:

```
sentence1 category1
sentence2 category2
...
```

This code will load the dataset, preprocess it, fit the Naive Bayes model on the labeled dataset, and then classify a new sentence. The predicted label will be printed to the console.

