To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple distance check between each planet before adding it to the array. If the distance between two planets is less than the desired minimum distance, you can generate a new random position for that planet until it meets the criteria. Here's an updated version of the `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        const distance = Math.sqrt(
          (coord - planet.position[index]) ** 2
        );
        return distance < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to call this function with the desired number of planets and minimum distance as arguments:

```javascript
setPlanets(generateRandomPlanets(5, 35));
```

Here's a React utility that removes widows from text. It uses the `wordWrap` property of the `style` object to wrap words within a container element, preventing them from extending beyond the container's width and creating widows.

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text, containerWidth }) => {
  const [wrappedText, setWrappedText] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    const wrapText = (text, containerWidth) => {
      const words = text.split(' ');
      const lines = [];

      for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
        const word = words[i];
        const lineWidth = containerWidth - 10; // Adjust the margin as needed

        if (word.length > lineWidth) {
          lines.push(word.slice(0, lineWidth));
          lines.push(word.slice(lineWidth));
        } else {
          lines.push(word);
        }
      }

      return lines.join(' ');
    };

    setWrappedText(wrapText(text, containerWidth));
  }, [text, containerWidth]);

  return (
    <div style={{ width: containerWidth, wordWrap: 'break-word' }}>
      {wrappedText}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, you can pass in the text and the desired container width like this:

```javascript
<RemoveWidows text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit." containerWidth={300} />
```

This will render the wrapped text within a container element with a width of 300 pixels, preventing widows from occurring.

