Here's a PowerShell script that recursively checks folders to see if their ACLs refer to SIDs which occur only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain:

```powershell
# Define the domain name
$domainName = "your_domain_name"

# Define the path to the folder you want to start checking
$startPath = "C:\path\to\folder"

# Function to check if a SID is present in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain
function CheckSIDInSIDHistory($sid) {
    $domain = Get-ADDomain -Filter {Name -eq $domainName}
    $users = Get-ADUser -Filter {Sid -eq $sid} -Properties MemberOf
    $groups = Get-ADGroup -Filter {Sid -eq $sid} -Properties MemberOf

    if ($users.Count -gt 0) {
        return True
    } elseif ($groups.Count -gt 0) {
        return True
    } else {
        return False
    }
}

# Function to recursively check folders for SIDs in SIDHistory
function CheckFolderForSIDsInSIDHistory($path) {
    Get-ChildItem -Path $path -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        $acl = (Get-Acl -Path $_.FullName).Access
        foreach ($access in $acl) {
            if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-') {
                $sid = $access.IdentityReference
                if (CheckSIDInSIDHistory($sid)) {
                    Write-Host "SID $sid found in SIDHistory in folder $_.FullName"
                }
            }
        }
        CheckFolderForSIDsInSIDHistory($_.FullName)
    }
}

# Call the function to start checking
CheckFolderForSIDsInSIDHistory($startPath)
```

Replace `your_domain_name` with the name of your domain. This script will recursively check all folders under the specified starting path and print out any SIDs found in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain.
